#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e7cd63-18d6-5233-bfcc-a6e3503563bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e7cd63-18d6-5233-bfcc-a6e3503563bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352665} :answer-field/id #uuid "887b1643-22d3-59de-80c6-88f0037de6f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba05b4b2-b3b1-53a1-bcca-7955722be825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(3t)-\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba05b4b2-b3b1-53a1-bcca-7955722be825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(3t)-\\sin(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352667} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f412035-eb98-55ac-ba19-08291c873da0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59c9f804-f9a4-5410-9757-ac34bf2e65f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-334634" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 3\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-3\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 3\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+3{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a118fe4a-cd1f-52f7-aa33-59f5494e4905" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Distinct Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e40781-9604-5585-b39f-741e4e4c3543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(4t)+2\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352670} {:answer/id #uuid "12c48251-3b55-5d9b-8a60-2657fa8fbeef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(4t)-2\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47e40781-9604-5585-b39f-741e4e4c3543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(4t)+2\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352670} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb407af6-b665-5fe1-ae8d-7c50ce3cfaec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1340cf-2bd8-57de-a514-fc57386b52d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(4t)-2\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b1340cf-2bd8-57de-a514-fc57386b52d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cos(4t)-2\\sin(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352673} :answer-field/id #uuid "de04c4f8-3bc6-5b89-b5b9-a17cc248d297" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94839ee0-f053-594f-8f02-8dbb5622b035" :question/math-academy-id "q-334635" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm 4\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-4\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(4\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot 4\\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-\\frac{1}{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352674 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fd1d280-c768-5504-892e-a9a1948e1f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix Exponent for a Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "843aa46f-2c43-5b7c-82d2-0d6edd88d2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "843aa46f-2c43-5b7c-82d2-0d6edd88d2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352676} :answer-field/id #uuid "05f88b08-23eb-5f8e-b21f-e80338fd392f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352675} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58eb0cfd-c30e-52c0-ab1d-c96ea77d345f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58eb0cfd-c30e-52c0-ab1d-c96ea77d345f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352678} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f9766f-4fe0-5d1e-87ca-87c89082c77e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a003aed5-aab8-5107-88cd-d01220dd3070" :question/math-academy-id "q-334729" :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $3t$ | $1-3t$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1+3t), \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3{e}^{3t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1+3t & -3t \\\\ 3t & 1-3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bdf36c2-f92f-58b6-aa5e-829fa85ce6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352681} {:answer/id #uuid "e556a325-6f9c-5ff8-af84-288d97f93668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e556a325-6f9c-5ff8-af84-288d97f93668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352682} :answer-field/id #uuid "235685d9-6853-5b58-aca8-f0b4bfd36a45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9560591b-10ed-5c6c-88a8-d66f2a0a73ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-2t)e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9560591b-10ed-5c6c-88a8-d66f2a0a73ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-2t)e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352684} :answer-field/id #uuid "64d5fed0-0710-56be-9a03-13fe8a3f7a6c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cff75a90-c5eb-5930-a12a-dc8b73dc18a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff75a90-c5eb-5930-a12a-dc8b73dc18a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352686} :answer-field/id #uuid "6193a998-8095-5b95-afcd-613a5e052c9d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e6895ba-1f35-583a-b183-c4a607030bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e6895ba-1f35-583a-b183-c4a607030bad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352688} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ebac8c6-7b20-5d88-baf6-685e092131f0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7f0acc0-0f4c-5e4b-878b-becd854f2c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-335093" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2t} & (1-2t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4fc3ba4-d488-5156-88dc-837369269cef" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee2ac0b-c