-5281-b39a-db4a719d9b07" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec4ec1fa-f063-5a3c-ba78-66239dbbdec2" :question/math-academy-id "q-335096" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -10 \\\\ 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t) & -10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t) \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10t) & -10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
